=========================================================
VW AND VH UNITS IN CSS (VIEWPORT UNITS)
=========================================================

Definition:
vw and vh are relative CSS units based on the viewport.

Viewport:
The visible area of the browser window (screen).

vw = viewport width
vh = viewport height

---------------------------------------------------------
BASIC MEANING
---------------------------------------------------------

1vw = 1% of the viewport width
1vh = 1% of the viewport height

These units do NOT depend on:
- Parent elements
- Root font size
- Content size

They depend ONLY on the viewport size.

---------------------------------------------------------
HOW VW WORKS
---------------------------------------------------------

vw is calculated based on the width of the viewport.

Example meaning:
- Viewport width = 1000px
- 1vw = 10px
- 50vw = 500px
- 100vw = full screen width

---------------------------------------------------------
HOW VH WORKS
---------------------------------------------------------

vh is calculated based on the height of the viewport.

Example meaning:
- Viewport height = 800px
- 1vh = 8px
- 50vh = 400px
- 100vh = full screen height

---------------------------------------------------------
COMMON USE CASES
---------------------------------------------------------

vw:
- Full-width sections
- Responsive font sizing
- Layout scaling based on screen width

vh:
- Full-height sections
- Hero banners
- Centering content vertically

---------------------------------------------------------
VW AND VH FOR FONT SIZE
---------------------------------------------------------

Font size can also be set using vw or vh.

Behavior:
- Text scales based on screen size
- Larger screen → larger text
- Smaller screen → smaller text

Caution:
Using vw/vh for text can cause readability issues
on very large or very small screens.

---------------------------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES OF VW & VH
---------------------------------------------------------

- Truly responsive
- Independent of parent elements
- Easy full-screen layouts
- No nesting issues like em

---------------------------------------------------------
DISADVANTAGES OF VW & VH
---------------------------------------------------------

- Can break layouts on mobile browsers
- Mobile address bar affects viewport height
- Not ideal for body text
- Requires careful testing

---------------------------------------------------------
VW VS VH (QUICK COMPARISON)
---------------------------------------------------------

vw:
- Based on viewport width
- Best for horizontal scaling

vh:
- Based on viewport height
- Best for vertical scaling

---------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT MOBILE NOTE
---------------------------------------------------------

On mobile devices:
- Browser UI (address bar) changes viewport size
- 100vh may cause overflow issues

Modern solution:
- Use newer units like dvh, svh, lvh (advanced topic)

---------------------------------------------------------
KEY TAKEAWAY
---------------------------------------------------------

vw and vh are viewport-based units.
They ignore parent and root elements.
They are best for layout sizing, not main text.

Use vw and vh carefully and intentionally.
